{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MuhE32qTfZ831g4Vviy4cN","type":"tool_result","content":"+++\nid = \"WI-1002\"\ntitle = \"The dispatcher's parked-lane resume leaves out a lane whose claimed row reads blocked, so a filed owner-held step launches no worker\"\nworkstream = \"process\"\nsr_refs = [\"SR-026\", \"SR-148\"]\nspecref = \"docs/reviews/wi-993/012-ADJUDICATE-26b4d09.md\"\nbuildtier = \"medium\"\nsafety_class = \"ordinary\"\nplanmode = \"none\"\npriority = 5\n+++\n\n## Context\n\nFiled on trunk at WI-993's landing, from the SUCCESSOR ruling of its gate's\ndispute sitting (`docs/reviews/wi-993/012-ADJUDICATE-26b4d09.md`, finding PARK,\nre-graded MINOR there). `dispatch._parked_branches` reads no schedule, so on the\nunattended route a claimed row citing a pending item is relaunched, against the\nworker brief's \"no worker assignment\". The state predates WI-993, which only\nsanctions it. The fix reads the `blocked` disposition LLR-288 already derives; it\nadds no second predicate and no detector for a session that stopped and filed\nnothing (WI-993's call 7). LLR-143 owes its amendment and sitting. Owes its scope\ncritique before its claim.\n\n## Done-when\n\n- [ ] The parked-lane resume leaves out a lane whose claimed row reads blocked in\n      the shared schedule: it launches no worker, keeps the claim, goes on to\n      claim the next ready row and ends without a stall. A parked lane whose\n      claimed row reads anything else keeps today's resume admission. The\n      promise reaches supported workflows and the kit's own routes, as\n      PROCESS.md §6 \"Review threat model\" bounds a spec's promise; hand-edited\n      kit-written state, hand-chosen aliases and sub-second timing windows are\n      outside it.\n- [ ] The resume LLR is amended to say so.\n- [ ] A `tests/test_dispatch.py` case over a parked blocked lane beside a ready\n      row asserts no launch, the claim kept and the next row claimed.\n- [ ] RESYNC entry, if the behaviour reaches adopters.\n- [ ] Review bar: A+B (REVIEW-A plus an independent REVIEW-B).\n\n## Critique dispositions\n\nRound 1 (fresh Codex session, 2026-11-07): CHANGES-REQUESTED, two MINORs.\n\n- **S3, the default for other dispositions and the supported-workflow bound:\n  adopted.** The first Done-when item now reads \"A parked lane whose claimed\n  row reads anything else keeps today's resume admission\", and it carries the\n  bound.\n- **S4, the planning ruling: adopted as `planmode = \"none\"`.** The change is\n  one filter on the parked-lane list, reading a disposition that already\n  exists, and the Done-when's `tests/test_dispatch.py` case fully specifies it,\n  which is S4's stated exception.\n\nRound 2 (fresh Codex session, confirming, 2026-11-07): APPROVE, no findings; both dispositions confirmed.\nReviewed: c873dcf8aade6179d893f6138adca8eefea9ab5c\n\n**Verified.** Full-lane review of `f28530899..c873dcf8a` (WI-1002, spec `docs/work/active/wi-1002/WI-1002-parked-resume-skips-blocked.md`, specref `docs/reviews/wi-993/012-ADJUDICATE-26b4d09.md`, rubric `docs/rubrics/kit-change-review.md`). I checked the worst failure classes first. (a) Silent wrong content: a lane wrongly left out, so a claim is stranded. `_parked_branches` (project-trajectory/scripts/dispatch.py:206-213) excludes a lane only when one of its trunk-claimed ids reads `blocked` in `schedule.evaluate`. I read `schedule._disposition` and `_held_disposition`: `blocked` arises only from an unmet open-item edge, pending or unknown, so a lane waiting on a WI predecessor still resumes. (b) Fail-open: a held row still launched. The only consumer is `_admit` (dispatch.py:1199), so the excluded lane cannot be launched through any other arm. (c) Data loss: the excluded lane keeps its active/ spec and branch, and no handback is written. I drove the real shipped path, `dispatch.run` over the stub harness. The new test `test_a_parked_blocked_lane_draws_no_worker_and_the_ready_row_is_claimed` FAILS on the pre-fix code. I checked this on a `git archive` copy of c873dcf with f28530899's dispatch.py restored: `AssertionError: [('wi-402', 'WI-402'), ('wi-401-widget', 'WI-401')]`. The characterization test `test_a_parked_lane_citing_a_ruled_item_still_resumes_first` passes on both trees. It would fail on the two plausible wrong implementations its comment names, exclude every parked lane or exclude every lane citing an OI. Done-when map: (1) the behaviour, both halves, is covered by the two new tests. The blocked case asserts no launch, the claim kept (spec in active/, branch ref present, no handback), the ready row claimed and merged, no STALL, and rc 0. (2) The LLR amendment is at docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml:1420, and per K1 it states the rule the right way round. (3) The `tests/test_dispatch.py` case is present, tagged through the module's `Implements: TC-137`, with TC-137 evidence = tests/test_dispatch.py. (4) The RESYNC entry is at project-trajectory/RESYNC_PACK.md:10658, in the house format, with no forced migration. (5) The A+B bar is process; this review is the B. Requirement/interface record: the changed side is LLR-143, and TC-137 carries the corresponding change. The counterparts are retained with justification. SR-026, the parent, is unchanged: \"resumes from its explicit claimed assignment\" still holds for every resumed lane, so its acceptance is preserved. LLR-288, the producer of `blocked`, is unchanged: its \"ruling the holding items releases the row with no edit\" is exactly what lets the excluded lane resume on the next run. LLR-061/agent_brief, which writes \"no worker assignment\", is unchanged and is now agreed with rather than contradicted. On the change-intake route, this is a scoped LLR amendment that preserves parent acceptance, not a workaround preserving an unsuitable LLR. Re-attestation of LLR-143's changed Approved text is still owed through its sitting, as the spec says, and no Status flipped (K3). K2: no SN or SR cell changed. Coverage-extension notes, not findings, all fail closed: a finished-but-round-owed lane whose trunk claim reads blocked is now left to the merge slot, which refuses it loudly. A hand-made two-spec claim with one blocked id is skipped rather than hitting the two-spec refusal; hand-edited state is outside the spec's bound.\n\n**Commands.**\n- `git rev-parse HEAD` -> `c873dcf8aade6179d893f6138adca8eefea9ab5c`\n- `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider -n 2 --basetemp .../wi1002-r1-B/wi-1002 <the 30 listed files>` -> `1147 passed, 5 skipped in 1391.77s (0:23:11)`\n- `python project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py --strict` -> `check_trajectory: clean (999 work item(s), 888 done (89%), 34 cancelled, graph acyclic).`\n- Pre-fix replay (git archive of c873dcf, dispatch.py from f28530899, in scratch): `pytest tests/test_dispatch.py -k \"parked_blocked or citing_a_ruled\"` -> `1 failed, 1 passed, 39 deselected in 31.23s` (the blocked case fails at its worker-calls assertion)\n- `git log -p --format=%h f28530899..c873dcf8a -- tests/test_dispatch.py project-trajectory/scripts/dispatch.py` (the source of finding 1)\n- `python -c \"import pathlib, sys\"` 0.116 s vs `python -c \"import pytest\"` 0.355 s (the cost in finding 1)\n\n**Findings.**\n- [MINOR] tests/test_dispatch.py:597 -> a stray `import pytest` sits inside the `STUB_CHECK` string, which is the stub `scripts/check.py` every drive fixture repo runs as its bar. The tests-first commit added it beside the real top-level import, and the final commit removed only the top-level one. The edit is unrelated to WI-1002. It makes the stub bar's red/green depend on pytest being importable in the interpreter that runs it, and it adds about 0.24 s of import to every stub-bar run in the suite (measured). B1: confirmed by the patch history and by timing -> delete the `import pytest` line and its following blank line from `STUB_CHECK` -> @owner\n- [MINOR] project-trajectory/scripts/dispatch.py:206 -> the read \"which rows does trunk's schedule hold `blocked`\" is now composed twice, in intent and almost byte for byte. Both `agent_brief.worker_prompt` (project-trajectory/scripts/agent_brief.py:205-213) and `_parked_branches` call `schedule.evaluate(schedule._load(root), oi_status=schedule.load_oi_status(root))` and filter `disposition == \"blocked\"`, and both reach through the private `schedule._load`. Two consumers of one hold will drift, for example if one later passes `reserved`. This repeats existing work (PROCESS.md §3, \"Consolidate, don't duplicate\") -> expose one public schedule reader of the held records (ids plus `open_items`) and have both the brief and the resume admission call it -> @owner\n- [MINOR] docs/test/test-cases.toml:1351 -> for clarity (K1): the added TC-137 sentence makes the lane the subject of \"claims the next ready row\", but the run claims it, not the lane. It also omits two things the test actually verifies: the run ends without a stall, and a parked lane whose row reads anything else, such as a ruled item, still resumes first, which is the second half of the amended LLR-143 -> reword to \"A parked lane whose claimed row reads blocked, beside a ready row, draws no worker and keeps its claim while the run claims and merges the ready row and ends without a stall; the same lane citing a ruled item resumes first\" -> @owner\n\nVERDICT: APPROVE findings=3","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62488e29-4f8f-46dc-a1ab-0647d367ffcd","uuid":"882a8f21-8ba2-4c21-8ec5-fb346857ed1f","timestamp":"2026-11-07T22:07:02.937Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"+++\nid = \"WI-1002\"\ntitle = \"The dispatcher's parked-lane resume leaves out a lane whose claimed row reads blocked, so a filed owner-held step launches no worker\"\nworkstream = \"process\"\nsr_refs = [\"SR-026\", \"SR-148\"]\nspecref = \"docs/reviews/wi-993/012-ADJUDICATE-26b4d09.md\"\nbuildtier = \"medium\"\nsafety_class = \"ordinary\"\nplanmode = \"none\"\npriority = 5\n+++