{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgpL7knRJdC9YQdBjZDza","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've committed sitting 003's verdict as `455b4d3ee`, in `docs/reviews/wi-1002/003-ADJUDICATE-8e5e544.md`. Both rows change meaning, and I would bless both. The commit hooks passed, and the commit carries the `WI: WI-1002` and `Loop-Session` trailers.\n\n**`VERDICT: MEANING rows=2`**\n\n- **LLR-143 Detail:** before, every parked lane resumed. Now a parked lane whose claimed row reads blocked in the shared schedule launches no worker and keeps its claim. The run then claims the next ready row, and the left-out lane doesn't count as a stall. Any other parked lane still resumes first, as before. Code written to the old text would relaunch the blocked lane, so this is a change of meaning. I would bless it:\n  - Its parent, SR-026, is still met, because every lane that does resume still resumes from its claimed assignment.\n  - It reuses the \"blocked\" state the schedule already computes rather than adding a second rule.\n  - It states both what changes and what stays the same, and adds no new code names.\n- **TC-137 Method:** it adds two checks. A blocked parked lane beside a ready row draws no worker and keeps its claim, while the run claims and merges the ready row and ends without a stall. The same lane, once the item it cites is ruled, resumes first. Both test functions exist in `tests/test_dispatch.py`. The Full tier matches that module being slow-only, and the wording now has the run, not the lane, doing the claiming, as reviewer B asked.\n\nBoth tiers are released and no other unblessed drifted row is in either registry, so the later act turn can re-attest both rows. I ran no test suites; this sitting rules on text."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":450,"cache_read_input_tokens":402875,"cache_creation":